What This Dataset Is About
In the U.S., the Senate votes on proposed laws (called bills).
For a bill to pass the Senate, it needs a majority vote (more than half of Senators voting “Yes”).

Every vote is recorded publicly. These are called roll call votes.
The data contains the voting records from the 114th Senate.

What Each Row and Column Means
Each row = one Senator
Each column (like 00001, 00004, etc.) = one bill that was voted on

What the Numbers Mean
Inside the table:
1 → Senator voted Yes
0 → Senator voted No
0.5 → Senator abstained (did not vote Yes or No)

Other Important Columns

name → The Senator’s name
party → The Senator’s political party
D = Democrat
R = Republican
I = Independent

Why This Is Useful for Clustering
Since each Senator has a pattern of Yes/No votes:
Senators who vote similarly will have similar numbers.
Clustering can group Senators based on how they vote.
This may show political groupings or voting behavior patterns.
